  "articleBody": "It's frustrating when your website, optimized for traditional search, seems invisible to AI tools like Google AI Overviews or ChatGPT. You're probably thinking, \"I've done all the SEO things, what gives?\" The truth is, AI search operates on a slightly different wavelength. It's not just about keywords and backlinks anymore; it's about structured data, crawlability, and direct answers.\n\nFirst, AI systems need to *access* your site. We've seen many businesses unintentionally block AI crawlers like OpenAI's OAI-SearchBot with their robots.txt files, assuming general search crawler rules apply. They don't always, and this can be a huge oversight. Next, your content needs to be *understandable* to AI. This means having clear, concise answers to specific questions, not just long-form articles full of jargon. Stuffing keywords or relying solely on text embedded in images or fancy JavaScript widgets will likely make your content invisible to AI.\n\nThen there's the structure. AI loves structured data (JSON-LD schema) that accurately reflects your on-page content. If your schema is missing, outdated, or doesn't match what's visible, AI systems will struggle to categorize and cite your information reliably. Finally, generic, AI-generated content without clear authorship or editorial review often gets overlooked because AI prioritizes fact density and semantic relevance. It's looking for real expertise and authority.\n\n**When Professional Guidance Helps**\n\nIf your site uses complex templates, heavy JavaScript rendering, or has recently undergone migrations, getting a professional audit can be a game-changer. They can pinpoint technical blocking issues that are preventing AI crawlers from accessing and understanding your content. This is especially true if organic search traffic is critical for your business. Identifying and fixing these issues often requires developer support for server or CMS changes, making expert intervention invaluable to ensure your site is truly open for AI discovery.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
